## Installing

The chart is published as an OCI artifact and signed with cosign. Its version is the same as the ThreatSea release it deploys.

```bash
helm install threatsea oci://ghcr.io/maibornwolff/charts/threatsea --version <version> \
  --namespace threatsea --create-namespace \
  --values my-values.yaml
```

Verify the signature before installing:

```bash
cosign verify ghcr.io/maibornwolff/charts/threatsea:<version> \
  --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github\.com/MaibornWolff/ThreatSea/\.github/workflows/release\.yml@refs/heads/(main|next)$' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
```

Pre-releases are published from the `next` branch. Pass `--devel` (or an explicit pre-release `--version`) to install one.

## Required values

| Value | Description |
| ----- | ----------- |
| `config.origin` | Externally reachable origin of ThreatSea, for example `https://threatsea.example.com`. The OIDC callback URL is `<origin>/api/auth/redirect` |
| `config.oidc.issuerUrl` | OIDC issuer URL |
| `config.oidc.clientId` | OIDC client ID |
| `secret.values.jwtSecret` | JWT signing secret |
| `secret.values.sessionSecret` | Express session secret |
| `secret.values.oidcClientSecret` | OIDC client secret |
| `secret.values.databasePassword` | Password for `database.user` on the external database |
| `database.host` | Hostname of the external PostgreSQL server (not needed with the bundled database) |
| `database.user` | User for the external PostgreSQL server (not needed with the bundled database, which defaults it to `threatsea`) |

Rendering fails with a message naming the missing value. When `secret.existingSecret` is set, the `secret.values.*` entries are not needed.

## Secrets

There are two ways to supply the secrets:

1. **Through values.** Set `secret.values.*` and the chart creates the Secret.
2. **Through an existing Secret.** Set `secret.existingSecret` to the name of a Secret you manage. The chart then creates no Secret and reads the entries named by `secret.keys` (by default `jwt-secret`, `session-secret`, `oidc-client-secret` and `database-password`).

> [!WARNING]
> Secrets passed through values are stored in plaintext in the Helm release Secret. They are visible to anyone who can read Secrets in the namespace or run `helm get values`. Use `secret.existingSecret` to avoid this, for example with a Secret managed by External Secrets Operator or Sealed Secrets.

The chart cannot look inside an existing Secret. If one of the keys is missing, Kubernetes does not start the container and the pod stays in `CreateContainerConfigError`. `kubectl describe pod` names the missing key in the pod events.

If your existing Secret also holds non-secret settings, for example because it is synced from a key vault, the chart can read them from there too. Set the matching `secret.configKeys.*` entry to the key name and leave the plain value empty; rendering fails when both are set: `oidcIssuerUrl`, `oidcClientId`, `databaseHost`, `databaseName` and `databaseUser` replace `config.oidc.issuerUrl`, `config.oidc.clientId`, `database.host`, `database.name` and `database.user`. The database keys cannot be combined with `postgresql.enabled=true`.

With `postgresql.enabled=true` the `database-password` key is not read. The bundled database's password comes from `postgresql.auth.*`.

```bash
kubectl create namespace threatsea

kubectl create secret generic threatsea-secrets --namespace threatsea \
  --from-literal=jwt-secret="$(openssl rand -hex 32)" \
  --from-literal=session-secret="$(openssl rand -hex 32)" \
  --from-literal=oidc-client-secret='<client secret>' \
  --from-literal=database-password='<database password>'

helm install threatsea oci://ghcr.io/maibornwolff/charts/threatsea --version <version> \
  --namespace threatsea \
  --set secret.existingSecret=threatsea-secrets \
  --values my-values.yaml
```

## External database

An external PostgreSQL server is the documented production setup. Point the chart at it with the `database.*` values:

```yaml
database:
  host: postgres.example.com
  port: 5432
  name: threatsea
  user: threatsea
  tls: verify
```

`database.tls` sets the TLS mode and accepts the same values as the backend's `DATABASE_TLS`: `verify` (the default) connects over TLS and verifies the server certificate, `no-verify` connects over TLS but accepts any certificate, for example from a connection pooler with a self-signed one, and `disabled` connects in plaintext. If the server certificate is signed by a private CA, mount the CA bundle with `extraVolumes` and `extraVolumeMounts` and point Node.js at it with `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`. The root filesystem is read-only, so the bundle has to come from a volume:

```yaml
extraVolumes:
  - name: db-ca
    secret:
      secretName: my-db-ca
extraVolumeMounts:
  - name: db-ca
    mountPath: /etc/ssl/db-ca
    readOnly: true
extraEnv:
  - name: NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS
    value: /etc/ssl/db-ca/ca.crt
```

`extraEnv` cannot redefine variables the chart manages itself, such as `DATABASE_TLS` or `PORT`; rendering fails if you try.

## Bundled database

> [!WARNING]
> `postgresql.enabled: true` deploys a PostgreSQL StatefulSet for **evaluation only**. It runs a single replica, has no backups, serves plaintext connections (no TLS), and has no supported upgrade path between PostgreSQL major versions. For production use an external database, for example a managed service or [CloudNativePG](https://cloudnative-pg.io/).

```yaml
postgresql:
  enabled: true
  auth:
    password: change-me
```

With the bundled database enabled, `database.host` is not used and `database.tls` is forced to `disabled`. The app always connects to port 5432 of the bundled Service, so `database.port` is ignored.

Things to know before relying on it, even for an evaluation:

- **Credentials are fixed on first start.** PostgreSQL initialises the data directory once. Changing the password (`postgresql.auth.password`, or the password key in `postgresql.auth.existingSecret` when that is set), `database.name` or `database.user` afterwards has no effect on the existing PVC. The app then uses the new values while the database keeps the old ones, so the app can no longer connect.
- **The PVC survives `helm uninstall`.** Reinstalling with a different password then fails authentication. Delete the PVC (`kubectl delete pvc -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=<release>`) to start fresh, which also deletes the data.
- **Changing the PostgreSQL major version starts an empty database.** Overriding `postgresql.image.tag` to another major version makes PostgreSQL use a new data directory, so the app sees a fresh, empty database. The old data stays on the volume under the previous major version's directory.
- **Storage settings are immutable.** `postgresql.persistence.enabled`, `size` and `storageClass` end up in the StatefulSet's `volumeClaimTemplates`, which Kubernetes does not allow to change on upgrade.

## Testing the release

`helm test <release>` runs a pod that logs in to the configured database with `psql`, using the chart's credentials, and runs `SELECT 1`, so a wrong password fails the test. It uses the same TLS mode as the app: `verify` checks the server certificate against the image's system CAs (`sslmode=verify-full`), `no-verify` requires TLS without checking the certificate (`sslmode=require`) and `disabled` connects in plaintext. If the app trusts a private CA through `extraEnv`/`extraVolumes`, the test pod does not, so `helm test` fails in `verify` mode. The pod pulls `postgresql.image` even when an external database is used.

## Additional backend settings

The backend reads a few settings the chart has no value for. Set them through `extraEnv`:

| Variable | Default | Description |
| -------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `OIDC_SCOPE` | `openid profile email` | Space-separated scopes requested from the identity provider. Must include `openid`, otherwise the app does not start. ThreatSea needs the `email` claim to identify users, so drop `email` only if your provider includes it without that scope. |
| `OIDC_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_EMAIL_LINKING` | `false` | By default, a login is refused unless the identity provider reports the email as verified (`email_verified`), because the email is used to create the account or link it to an existing one. Set to `true` only for a provider that does not send `email_verified` and whose email addresses users cannot choose themselves; otherwise a user can take over another account by claiming its email. |
| `USER_PURGE_ENABLED` | `false` | Set to `true` to permanently delete users who have not logged in for `USER_PURGE_THRESHOLD_DAYS`. Users who are the only owner of a project or catalog are kept. Any other value leaves the purge disabled. Each pod runs the purge on startup and then every `USER_PURGE_INTERVAL_HOURS`. |
| `USER_PURGE_THRESHOLD_DAYS` | `365` | Days since the last login after which the purge deletes a user. Must be greater than `USER_HIDE_THRESHOLD_DAYS`. |
| `USER_PURGE_INTERVAL_HOURS` | `24` | Hours between purge runs, at most 596. |
| `USER_HIDE_THRESHOLD_DAYS` | `90` | Days since the last login after which a user is no longer offered when adding members to a project or catalog. Existing memberships stay visible. Applies even when the purge is disabled. |

```yaml
extraEnv:
  - name: USER_PURGE_ENABLED
    value: "true"
  - name: USER_PURGE_THRESHOLD_DAYS
    value: "180"
```

Invalid values (a non-positive number, or a hide threshold that is not smaller than the purge threshold) stop the app at startup, and the pod goes into `CrashLoopBackOff`.

